1
00:00:35,952 --> 00:00:38,413
<i>La Morte Bianca is looking for them.</i>

2
00:00:41,499 --> 00:00:42,667
Should we go to her?

3
00:00:42,751 --> 00:00:44,961
The police still see us as the enemy.

4
00:00:45,044 --> 00:00:47,088
This is exactly what I feared.

5
00:00:47,172 --> 00:00:51,718
That one day we'd have to choose
between the Concordat and Chiara.

6
00:00:55,221 --> 00:00:58,308
So why did you date
a mafia boss's daughter?

7
00:00:58,391 --> 00:00:59,601
I don't know.

8
00:01:00,852 --> 00:01:02,687
I guess that's what fate
had in store for me.

9
00:01:02,771 --> 00:01:05,523
That's right. We may not have control
over everything,

10
00:01:05,607 --> 00:01:06,941
but we always have a choice.

11
00:01:11,154 --> 00:01:12,197
Where's Cocoy?

12
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:13,239
He's taking care of something.

13
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:14,240
Doing what?

14
00:01:20,371 --> 00:01:22,207
<i>Cocoy, what I'm asking you to do</i>
<i>isn't illegal.</i>

15
00:01:22,290 --> 00:01:25,293
It just doesn't have
the agency's permission.

16
00:01:26,169 --> 00:01:29,631
And most importantly,
the offer is substantial.

17
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:34,052
MORAVIANO
CZECH REPUBLIC

18
00:01:45,730 --> 00:01:48,942
Specifically about the history
of the Concordat.

19
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:57,617
What is happening, Viktor?

20
00:01:59,494 --> 00:02:00,703
What’s happening?!

21
00:02:29,023 --> 00:02:30,108
That…

22
00:02:30,191 --> 00:02:35,405
like most leaders,
they get blinded by power.

23
00:02:35,488 --> 00:02:38,992
These elite leaders,
when pushed to their limits,

24
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:42,203
will literally turn against each other.

25
00:02:42,287 --> 00:02:48,668
So basically, they're like toddlers
fighting in high chairs?

26
00:02:50,003 --> 00:02:52,755
That was kind of
a weird comparison, though.

27
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:04,434
You know, I was finally
ready to trust you.

28
00:03:05,143 --> 00:03:06,978
In fact, I already did.

29
00:03:13,067 --> 00:03:15,320
He didn't fully agree,
but we reached a compromise.

30
00:03:18,197 --> 00:03:20,450
I was even willing to go
with you if necessary.

31
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:22,201
But then what?

32
00:03:25,246 --> 00:03:27,081
You know, Chiara, I get it.

33
00:03:27,165 --> 00:03:29,083
I get that you're used
to doing everything alone.

34
00:03:29,167 --> 00:03:31,419
That you're used to calling all the shots.

35
00:03:32,253 --> 00:03:34,464
And I also know
you've had a difficult past.

36
00:03:36,299 --> 00:03:37,634
But every time I try,

37
00:03:38,218 --> 00:03:39,761
there's always something new.

38
00:03:39,844 --> 00:03:41,763
A new plan, a new move.

39
00:03:41,846 --> 00:03:43,806
And we're just supposed to keep up?

40
00:03:45,058 --> 00:03:47,435
Because you're too slow, Alex.

41
00:03:48,019 --> 00:03:49,604
While you're holding meetings,

42
00:03:49,687 --> 00:03:52,232
they're already raiding the islands.

43
00:03:52,315 --> 00:03:54,609
While you're waiting for approvals,

44
00:03:54,692 --> 00:03:58,529
people are already losing
their lives and their homes.

45
00:03:58,613 --> 00:04:02,033
And while you're busy debating
whether something can be done,

46
00:04:02,116 --> 00:04:03,826
they're already making their move.

47
00:04:04,410 --> 00:04:06,621
Didn't it ever occur to you
that there might be a reason for that?

48
00:04:07,747 --> 00:04:09,290
Alex, we can no longer afford

49
00:04:09,374 --> 00:04:11,793
to sit around trying to figure out
what their reasons are.

50
00:04:14,045 --> 00:04:15,463
You're not a team player.

51
00:04:23,513 --> 00:04:25,890
Every leader starts out as part of a team.

52
00:04:42,699 --> 00:04:44,325
That's why I did something.

53
00:05:29,078 --> 00:05:30,663
La Fornarina.

54
00:05:39,255 --> 00:05:40,173
They refused.

55
00:05:42,133 --> 00:05:43,551
They told me to wait.

56
00:05:45,887 --> 00:05:50,558
So I found another way,
because honestly, Alex,

57
00:05:58,066 --> 00:05:59,984
We weren't even part of the decision?

58
00:06:00,068 --> 00:06:02,278
We don't need permission anymore, Alex.

59
00:06:04,072 --> 00:06:05,698
That's the problem.

60
00:06:06,199 --> 00:06:08,701
You don't need other people
until you suddenly do.

61
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:10,453
And by then,

62
00:06:10,536 --> 00:06:13,122
we're the ones left to clean up.
We're the ones who extract.

63
00:08:05,485 --> 00:08:06,777
Los Bandidos.

64
00:08:06,861 --> 00:08:09,155
LOS BANDIDOS

65
00:08:19,624 --> 00:08:20,708
I really don't get it.

66
00:08:20,791 --> 00:08:23,794
Why keep us in the dark
when there's a plan?

67
00:08:23,878 --> 00:08:26,214
Instead, there's always a secret mission,
and we're just left surprised?

68
00:08:26,297 --> 00:08:27,340
Left hanging?

69
00:08:28,299 --> 00:08:30,760
We're a team,
so why do we always feel like extras?

70
00:08:36,807 --> 00:08:37,642
Agent Dara.

71
00:08:38,392 --> 00:08:42,605
Tell me. Is it normal for former assassins
to be allergic to teamwork?

72
00:08:45,191 --> 00:08:47,485
Agent Alex, I just need to…

73
00:08:47,568 --> 00:08:49,195
- Hold on.
- <i>No, seriously.</i>

74
00:08:49,779 --> 00:08:51,948
I was finally starting to trust her.

75
00:08:52,031 --> 00:08:54,075
Actually, I already did.

76
00:08:54,158 --> 00:08:55,701
Then she goes off on another side quest.

77
00:08:57,245 --> 00:08:59,121
- But--
- <i>I'm not saying she's wrong, but…</i>

78
00:08:59,956 --> 00:09:02,208
Would it really hurt
to include us once in a while?

79
00:09:02,291 --> 00:09:05,211
Because if things go south,
we're the ones who deal with it.

80
00:09:05,294 --> 00:09:07,964
And isn't it exhausting that
we already have enemies out there,

81
00:09:08,047 --> 00:09:09,549
then we're clashing within the team too?

82
00:09:09,632 --> 00:09:10,591
It's exhausting.

83
00:09:13,427 --> 00:09:16,472
Because we have another small problem.

84
00:09:18,474 --> 00:09:21,435
Looks like Cocoy has already left
on an international route.

85
00:09:22,812 --> 00:09:25,481
We got a signal overseas.
Looks like Singapore.

86
00:09:26,148 --> 00:09:28,943
Agent Dara, tell me
General Norberto doesn't know yet.

87
00:09:29,652 --> 00:09:30,528
Not yet.

88
00:09:30,611 --> 00:09:31,654
Okay.

89
00:09:31,737 --> 00:09:34,365
Please don't let this reach him just yet.

90
00:09:34,448 --> 00:09:37,243
We just got out of hot water.
I don't want another scolding.

91
00:09:37,326 --> 00:09:38,744
Okay, got it.

92
00:09:38,828 --> 00:09:41,247
This won't get to the general.

93
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,332
Just take a breather, Agent A.

94
00:09:43,416 --> 00:09:46,502
If I find anything, I'll keep you posted.

95
00:09:51,882 --> 00:09:54,677
What wasn't the general
supposed to hear about?

96
00:09:56,345 --> 00:09:58,472
How are you? Had breakfast yet?

97
00:09:59,432 --> 00:10:01,559
Is there something I should know?

98
00:11:35,236 --> 00:11:36,529
My child.

99
00:11:36,612 --> 00:11:38,072
It's just garlic.

100
00:11:38,155 --> 00:11:40,783
It's not La Morte Bianca.

101
00:11:48,958 --> 00:11:51,335
You know, sweetheart, I understand you.

102
00:11:51,419 --> 00:11:55,256
Of course you're worried
about your mom and dad

103
00:11:55,339 --> 00:12:01,429
and about the disaster
that La Morte Bianca is planning.

104
00:12:04,181 --> 00:12:06,350
I also know you want to take action,

105
00:12:06,434 --> 00:12:09,270
so you know something's
always being done, right?

106
00:12:25,244 --> 00:12:26,120
Are you blowing bubbles?

107
00:12:28,164 --> 00:12:28,998
No, I'm not!

108
00:12:33,043 --> 00:12:34,628
Did you know?

109
00:12:34,712 --> 00:12:39,008
Your Uncle Mario
used to work in the service.

110
00:12:40,843 --> 00:12:41,927
Really?

111
00:12:42,011 --> 00:12:43,220
Yes.

112
00:12:43,304 --> 00:12:44,638
Why did he leave?

113
00:12:45,222 --> 00:12:48,767
Because Mario was hardheaded.

114
00:12:48,851 --> 00:12:49,852
Just like you.

115
00:12:50,436 --> 00:12:51,687
A little troublemaker.

116
00:12:52,605 --> 00:12:55,524
To him, there were just too many policies.

117
00:12:55,608 --> 00:12:58,277
There were more meetings
than actual action.

118
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:00,112
Everything was so slow.

119
00:13:02,072 --> 00:13:04,366
I get why he felt that way.

120
00:13:05,117 --> 00:13:06,994
It really is frustrating

121
00:13:07,495 --> 00:13:11,207
when all you do is follow orders,
while the problems keep piling up.

122
00:13:12,374 --> 00:13:13,542
But…

123
00:13:14,376 --> 00:13:16,879
there are things we don't always see.

124
00:13:16,962 --> 00:13:19,340
The consequences.

125
00:13:19,423 --> 00:13:22,426
The people who get caught
in the crossfire.

126
00:13:23,010 --> 00:13:25,554
The bigger picture.

127
00:13:28,891 --> 00:13:31,393
I grew up in a world

128
00:13:32,186 --> 00:13:36,148
where the powerful faced no consequences.

129
00:13:38,651 --> 00:13:41,529
If they wanted something, they got it.

130
00:13:41,612 --> 00:13:43,322
If they didn't want someone,

131
00:13:43,405 --> 00:13:44,698
they disappeared.

132
00:13:44,782 --> 00:13:47,409
Anyone caught in the middle
was just collateral.

133
00:13:50,329 --> 00:13:54,291
I know how dangerous they are,
all of them.

134
00:13:55,125 --> 00:13:57,294
I just don't want anyone getting hurt.

135
00:13:58,170 --> 00:14:04,176
Sweetheart, I know
no one wants anyone to get hurt.

136
00:14:04,802 --> 00:14:08,931
But you don't always
have to be the one taking action.

137
00:14:10,182 --> 00:14:16,188
Sometimes, a team moves faster
than a lone hero.

138
00:14:28,117 --> 00:14:30,494
I actually think
what you're saying is valid.

139
00:14:30,578 --> 00:14:31,537
It's true.

140
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:33,581
Chiara can be really difficult
to work with.

141
00:14:34,290 --> 00:14:35,124
Slight?

142
00:14:35,207 --> 00:14:36,166
Very!

143
00:14:36,250 --> 00:14:37,835
She's extremely independent.

144
00:14:37,918 --> 00:14:39,920
She's always in survival mode.

145
00:14:42,339 --> 00:14:43,632
That's probably her mantra.

146
00:14:43,716 --> 00:14:46,427
And whenever there's danger,
her first thought is,

147
00:14:46,510 --> 00:14:48,637
"I'll handle it. Me."

148
00:14:48,721 --> 00:14:50,180
You know?

149
00:14:50,264 --> 00:14:53,183
She's just not used to dragging
other people into her problems.

150
00:14:53,684 --> 00:14:56,770
I guess that's how
a world-class assassin thinks.

151
00:14:57,354 --> 00:14:59,148
But that doesn't make it right.

152
00:15:01,191 --> 00:15:03,402
Still, maybe she's worth hearing out.

153
00:15:04,445 --> 00:15:06,196
Maybe this time,

154
00:15:06,906 --> 00:15:08,365
she has a point too.

155
00:15:08,866 --> 00:15:09,700
Don't you think?

156
00:15:15,706 --> 00:15:17,166
What makes you say that?

157
00:15:23,631 --> 00:15:25,466
You trust me, don't you?

158
00:15:26,842 --> 00:15:27,968
Of course I do.

159
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:32,681
Wait. Why?

160
00:15:39,355 --> 00:15:41,857
A plan? One Alex doesn't know about?

161
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:44,735
Sweetheart! Here you go again.

162
00:15:44,818 --> 00:15:46,862
Didn't we just talk about this earlier?

163
00:15:54,161 --> 00:15:57,748
You mean people like La Fornarina?
Is that it?

164
00:15:57,831 --> 00:16:00,960
Yes. Former Communicados.

165
00:16:01,043 --> 00:16:03,045
Former assassins.

166
00:16:10,386 --> 00:16:13,222
Wow… your plan is making me nervous.

167
00:16:24,483 --> 00:16:27,152
I'm just not sure if I can
bring them all back,

168
00:16:27,236 --> 00:16:30,322
since they're scattered
all over the world.

169
00:16:30,406 --> 00:16:34,159
But with their skills, it's worth a shot.

170
00:16:38,789 --> 00:16:42,584
You know, sweetheart,
I still don't fully understand your plan.

171
00:16:42,668 --> 00:16:47,131
But because I love you,
of course, I'll support you.

172
00:16:49,717 --> 00:16:50,634
Wait, wait, wait.

173
00:16:51,218 --> 00:16:52,928
But first thing tomorrow,

174
00:16:53,012 --> 00:16:55,347
Alex has to know about this. Okay?

175
00:16:59,059 --> 00:17:02,730
You'll be Mr. Good Boy,
and I'll be Ms. Troublemaker.

176
00:17:03,439 --> 00:17:06,442
Yes, like good cop, bad cop.

177
00:17:06,525 --> 00:17:07,401
Like that.

178
00:17:16,326 --> 00:17:19,455
Cooperating as Ms. Troublemaker.

179
00:17:21,790 --> 00:17:22,624
So…

180
00:17:22,708 --> 00:17:27,796
When are you planning to reconnect
with the Los Bandidos?

181
00:17:29,965 --> 00:17:31,508
Actually, I've already started.

182
00:17:42,603 --> 00:17:43,645
I knew it!

183
00:17:43,729 --> 00:17:45,898
- Okay.
- Now talk Los Bandidos!

184
00:17:45,981 --> 00:17:48,025
I get you, but listen, okay?

185
00:17:48,108 --> 00:17:48,984
Just hear me out.

186
00:17:50,986 --> 00:17:52,362
They're former assassins.

187
00:17:52,446 --> 00:17:53,697
Former Concordat members.

188
00:17:53,781 --> 00:17:54,948
And according to Chiara,

189
00:17:55,032 --> 00:17:57,659
if anyone knows how they operate,
it's them!

190
00:17:58,577 --> 00:18:03,373
So if we want to catch up to,
outsmart, and take down our enemy…

191
00:18:03,457 --> 00:18:05,667
La Morte Bianca and the Concordat.

192
00:18:05,751 --> 00:18:09,129
Then Chiara believes,
and I believe it too,

193
00:18:09,213 --> 00:18:11,673
that the Los Bandidos
could be a huge help.

194
00:18:11,757 --> 00:18:14,676
They might just be our shortcut
and our wild card!

195
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:18,764
I'm not saying I like the approach, but…

196
00:18:18,847 --> 00:18:20,057
I get the logic.

197
00:18:28,107 --> 00:18:29,399
Agent Alex.

198
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:33,028
Maybe… is there something I can do?

199
00:18:34,738 --> 00:18:35,823
Officially, I mean.

200
00:18:35,906 --> 00:18:37,533
Not by going rogue.

201
00:18:37,616 --> 00:18:39,493
Not sneaking around.

202
00:18:39,576 --> 00:18:41,578
You know what I mean.

203
00:18:42,162 --> 00:18:44,915
Something that could actually help.

204
00:18:45,707 --> 00:18:47,793
I want to be useful to the agency again.

205
00:18:48,544 --> 00:18:50,337
I want to help you all again.

206
00:18:51,505 --> 00:18:52,673
You.

207
00:18:52,756 --> 00:18:55,050
At least make up for everything…

208
00:18:55,134 --> 00:18:56,093
You want to help?

209
00:18:57,302 --> 00:18:58,220
I do.

210
00:18:58,303 --> 00:19:00,514
In any way I can.

211
00:19:01,390 --> 00:19:04,101
For the task force,
for the operation, anything.

212
00:19:10,732 --> 00:19:12,317
Mikoy!

213
00:19:15,779 --> 00:19:18,282
Actually, there is something
you can do here at the safehouse.

214
00:19:19,199 --> 00:19:20,159
Really?

215
00:19:25,414 --> 00:19:27,332
Now that our team has grown,

216
00:19:27,416 --> 00:19:29,501
I need someone who can
keep things under control

217
00:19:29,585 --> 00:19:31,211
and manage situations.

218
00:19:33,881 --> 00:19:35,215
throughout the safehouse.

219
00:19:35,299 --> 00:19:38,260
And I think you're perfect for the job.

220
00:19:39,511 --> 00:19:41,555
Take charge. Take charge!

221
00:19:41,638 --> 00:19:44,057
Let's hurry up!

222
00:19:44,141 --> 00:19:46,268
Like being the class sergeant-at-arms?

223
00:19:46,351 --> 00:19:47,561
Like that?

224
00:19:47,644 --> 00:19:49,229
Come on, Agent Alex, I'm serious.

225
00:19:49,313 --> 00:19:51,356
Give me something a little cooler.

226
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:53,442
I'm serious too, Mikoy.

227
00:19:53,525 --> 00:19:56,695
I'm practically babysitting
this bunch of goofballs.

228
00:19:56,778 --> 00:19:58,280
It just feels too small.

229
00:19:58,363 --> 00:19:59,698
It's not.

230
00:20:00,282 --> 00:20:02,201
Not everyone who helps
is part of the operation.

231
00:20:02,284 --> 00:20:03,202
Not everyone carries a gun.

232
00:20:03,285 --> 00:20:05,746
And not everyone needs
to be out in the field.

233
00:20:07,289 --> 00:20:08,290
And honestly,

234
00:20:08,373 --> 00:20:12,461
if you'd just cooperate
instead of enabling Chiara,

235
00:20:12,544 --> 00:20:14,296
you could've helped out even more.

236
00:20:15,714 --> 00:20:16,840
Ouch.

237
00:20:25,057 --> 00:20:26,850
The Los Bandidos.

238
00:20:26,934 --> 00:20:29,394
The question is… Where are they?

239
00:20:29,478 --> 00:20:32,189
If we knew that,
we wouldn't still be here.

240
00:20:33,690 --> 00:20:34,942
Exactly!

241
00:20:35,025 --> 00:20:36,443
The problem is,

242
00:20:36,526 --> 00:20:38,904
they're elite former assassins.

243
00:20:40,322 --> 00:20:43,909
They're used to constantly
changing their identities.

244
00:20:43,992 --> 00:20:47,746
I think they probably haven't
completely disappeared yet.

245
00:20:47,829 --> 00:20:50,249
We just need to find more connections.

246
00:20:50,332 --> 00:20:52,584
What if they have a group chat?

247
00:20:52,668 --> 00:20:53,752
Right?

248
00:20:53,835 --> 00:20:56,672
Even my high school friends
still have our group chat.

249
00:20:56,755 --> 00:21:00,008
If they've truly left that life behind,

250
00:21:00,092 --> 00:21:01,969
why would they still keep in touch?

251
00:21:03,553 --> 00:21:04,429
Well…

252
00:21:04,513 --> 00:21:07,933
I still think we should follow Cocoy.

253
00:21:08,016 --> 00:21:10,185
And where exactly
are you going to find Cocoy?

254
00:21:10,269 --> 00:21:11,311
GLOBAL AGENCY FOR GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS

255
00:21:20,862 --> 00:21:23,323
Where are you planning
to track Cocoy down?

256
00:21:24,658 --> 00:21:26,660
Coconut jam, sir. Jam.

257
00:21:28,495 --> 00:21:30,205
Cocoy jam, sir.

258
00:21:31,164 --> 00:21:32,249
For your bread, sir.

259
00:21:32,874 --> 00:21:36,128
- Yes, sir.
- The team's just craving some, sir.

260
00:21:36,211 --> 00:21:37,421
Didn't you hear?

261
00:21:37,504 --> 00:21:40,048
They said it's a limited edition.

262
00:21:42,676 --> 00:21:43,677
- Yes, sir.
- Right…

263
00:21:43,760 --> 00:21:44,803
- It might sell out.
- Right, sir.

264
00:21:44,886 --> 00:21:46,638
Are you all hallucinating?

265
00:21:48,181 --> 00:21:53,395
You're researching
an international crime organization,

266
00:21:53,478 --> 00:21:55,147
and you're doing it on an empty stomach?

267
00:21:55,230 --> 00:21:57,691
How do you expect your brains to work?

268
00:22:03,530 --> 00:22:06,199
If I hear the phrase

269
00:22:06,283 --> 00:22:08,618
"Follow Cocoy"

270
00:22:09,786 --> 00:22:11,872
one more time,
I'll have all of you drug-tested.

271
00:22:13,707 --> 00:22:14,916
Dara!

272
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:17,961
Come to my office later.

273
00:22:24,551 --> 00:22:25,427
You, really!

274
00:22:25,510 --> 00:22:27,763
Where are we even supposed
to get cocoy jam?

275
00:22:27,846 --> 00:22:30,057
Where did you even hear about cocoy jam?

276
00:22:30,140 --> 00:22:31,767
Where will we get a cocoy jam?

277
00:22:52,621 --> 00:22:53,955
Who, sir?

278
00:22:54,706 --> 00:22:56,124
Senator Quintin.

279
00:23:24,152 --> 00:23:25,362
<i>How unfair.</i>

280
00:23:28,865 --> 00:23:30,826
I should be used of this kind of life.

281
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:34,204
This is still my hometown.

282
00:23:34,287 --> 00:23:36,748
<i>People are dying because of this mess.</i>

283
00:23:38,250 --> 00:23:40,460
I kind of miss the simple life.

284
00:23:53,849 --> 00:23:56,560
Wait, it looks like… he has so many looks.

285
00:23:56,643 --> 00:23:59,604
Actually… He seems friend with everybody.

286
00:24:06,278 --> 00:24:08,405
What else I did not notice?

287
00:24:08,488 --> 00:24:12,200
<i>What are you hiding, Senator Quintin?</i>

288
00:24:17,205 --> 00:24:19,207
<i>Cocoy left the safehouse.</i>

289
00:24:19,291 --> 00:24:23,253
Unauthorized. International route.
And we only knew right after.
